<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mercenary">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercenary</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mercenary is a soldier who fights for pay in the service of a foreign power. Many empires relied on mercenaries, including the Achaemenid, Ptolemaic and Seleucid empires and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, whose army was largely made up of hired troops. Dependence on mercenaries could bring loyalty problems, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercenary.jpg" alt="Mercenary" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Hans Holbein the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Swiss_Mercenaries,_design_for_a_Stained_Glass_Window,_workshop_of_Hans_Holbein_the_Younger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A mercenary, also called a soldier of fortune, is a private individual who joins an armed conflict for personal profit, is otherwise an outsider to the conflict and is not a member of any other official military. Mercenaries fight for money or other forms of payment rather than for political interests. Whether a person counts as a mercenary may be a matter of degree, since financial and political interests can overlap.</p><p>Article 47 of the 1977 Protocol Additional to the Geneva Conventions gives the most widely accepted international definition. It lists conditions such as being specially recruited to fight, taking a direct part in hostilities, being motivated essentially by private gain, and being neither a national of a party to the conflict nor a member of its armed forces. A mercenary has no right to be a combatant or a prisoner of war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Hired soldiers appear in the earliest records. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is said to have used 11,000 mercenaries in his battles in the thirteenth century BCE, and Egypt long recruited foreign corps such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjay">Medjay</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. In 401 BCE Cyrus the Younger hired a large army of Greek mercenaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a>, to seize the Persian throne from his brother Artaxerxes II. After Cyrus died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cunaxa">Battle of Cunaxa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> helped lead the survivors north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, as he recounts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>. Carthage was unusual in relying primarily on mercenaries.</p><p>Kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> regularly employed them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> hired Tibetan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> soldiers against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the Saika group of Kii Province impeded the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> during the Siege of Ishiyama Hongan-ji between 1570 and 1580. In Nicaragua in 1855 the American William Walker, hired by the Liberals, defeated the Conservatives at the Battle of La Virgen and became the country's de facto ruler. By 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> had imported about 400 French mercenaries to train his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From the twentieth century mercenaries came to be seen as less entitled to protection under the rules of war. Foreign nationals who serve in a state's regular forces, such as the Gurkha units of the British and Indian armies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion">French Foreign Legion</a>, are not classed as mercenaries under Article 47, because they belong to its armed forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mercenary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league's army was a citizen militia drawn from the member cities, supplemented by <b>mercenaries</b> paid from federal funds, with a cavalry force under a federal hipparch and a small fleet under a navarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Revolt of Cyrus the Younger (401 BCE) a Persian prince hired Greek <b>mercenaries</b> to contest the throne and died at Cunaxa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Alans served as allies and <b>mercenaries</b> of both Rome and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almohad military was built on the Masmuda Berber tribes that had gathered around Ibn Tumart's movement, supplemented by Zanata and Arab cavalry, Andalusian contingents and later Christian <b>mercenaries</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As this elite aged, it was supplemented by Christian <b>mercenaries</b> under commanders such as the Catalan Reverter and by Andalusian levies, and the army lost the character and the edge it had begun with.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The core of Antigonid power was the Macedonian phalanx, a dense formation of infantry armed with the sarissa, a pike some five to six metres long, supported by peltasts, cavalry and <b>mercenaries</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
